{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfBSZFYacDwHXNjJfFip2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted **index 0 (In 0.87 / Sb 0.13)** as the best composition. At 0.112%, it had the highest external quantum efficiency (EQE, the merit for this library) of the three compositions I measured.\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 15 | 0.51 | 0.053 |\n| 5 | 0.20 | 0.073 |\n| 0 | 0.13 | 0.112 |\n\n- **What I measured:** there were only three merit measurements for 28 compositions. I spent the first on the 50:50 point (index 15, near the compound InSbO₄) and the second on an In-rich point (index 5). EQE was higher at index 5, so I used the last one on index 0, the most In-rich composition available.\n- **Trend:** EQE rose steadily as the Sb fraction fell. Since index 0 is the end of the library, nothing more In-rich could beat it.\n- **What's untested:** I never measured the Sb-rich end (indices 20–27). I gave it low priority because EQE was falling as Sb increased, but that is an extrapolation from three points. If that end has a separate high-EQE phase, I would have missed it.\n- **Unused tools:** I didn't use any of the five XRD measurements or the phase-matching tools, so the phases present were not identified. The choice rests only on the three EQE values."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":423,"cache_read_input_tokens":7870,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":423},"output_tokens":456,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc0bd388-19b9-4525-a42d-4832ede9c2f7","uuid":"cf6a4109-453c-4c3a-ae20-5d689245de54","timestamp":"2026-09-18T19:16:35.559Z","request_id":"req_011CfBSZF8GZVk4GHJsa3AiL"}
